      "She was breathing. She possessed a pulse. She had a signature on a marriage contract. For twenty-one years, Ma’un had operated under the assumption that women existed in two categories: family members who yelled at him for leaving socks on the radiator, and strangers he diligently avoided making eye contact with on the street. Now, one was standing in his hallway, carrying a handbag, expected to stay forever.",
