      "Before Naba could say another word, the downstairs doorbell rang.",
      "It didn't ring politely. It buzzed three times in loud, impatient bursts.",
      "Footsteps raced up the fourteen stairs, two at a time. The door swung open, and a bright, energetic voice echoed into the hallway:",
      "“Where is my big sister?!”",
      "It was Sara, Naba’s sixteen-year-old half-sister. She threw her coat onto a chair, ran across the room, and wrapped her arms around Naba’s waist in a tight bear hug.",
      "Naba hugged her back, a genuine, relaxed smile touching her face.",
      "“Father is at a business conference until tomorrow,” Sara announced, kicking off her boots and sitting on the carpet. “So I took a cab straight here. And I brought pastries.”",
      "Within ten minutes, Sara was devouring a cream roll while Cookie sniffed her socks.",
      "Ma’un looked at Naba. “Earlier, before the bell… you were about to tell me something.”",
      "Sara stopped mid-chew. She looked at Ma’un, then at Naba, her eyes wide.",
      "“Wait,” Sara said, dropping the pastry onto her napkin. “Hold on. You still haven't told him anything?!”",
      "“Sara, please,” Naba said quietly. “It’s fine.”",
      "“It’s not fine!” Sara crossed her arms, turning directly to Ma’un. “He’s your husband. He needs to know what kind of house you grew up in.”",
      "Sara looked Ma’un dead in the eye. All her childish teasing disappeared.",
      "“Sit down, Ma’un. You need to hear this.”",
      "Ma’un sat back against the wooden floorboards.",
      "“First,” Sara said, “Naba and I have different mothers. Her mother was Maryam. My father married Maryam for family business. There was no love there. When Naba was five, Maryam got sick. Really sick. The servants whispered that she was being poisoned, but nobody investigated. Maryam died in a hospital bed on a Tuesday. Three months later, my father brought my mother into the house.”",
      "Ma’un felt a knot tighten in his stomach.",
      "“Naba looked just like her mother,” Sara continued, her voice bitter. “Same eyes, same quiet manner. My mother hated her for it. She took Naba’s bedroom and turned it into my playroom. She pulled Naba out of private school, fired the maids, and made Naba do all the housework. If a dish had a watermark, Naba was yelled at. If dinner was five minutes late, Naba didn’t get to eat.”",
      "Sara looked down at her hands, ashamed.",
      "“And I was the spoiled princess. Imported clothes, tutors, private lessons. For years, I just ignored her. I walked past her while she scrubbed floors on her knees. But Naba never got angry at me. When I had nightmares, she would sneak into my room, hold my hand, and recite Quran until I fell asleep.”",
      "Sara took a shaky breath, tears welling in her eyes.",
      "“The worst thing happened when Naba was fourteen. It was winter, freezing cold outside. Naba spent three weeks secretly knitting mufflers from scrap wool—a cream one for my mother, and a pink one for me. She stayed up after midnight with bleeding, calloused fingers so nobody would see her.”",
      "Sara wiped a tear with her sleeve.",
      "“On a freezing evening, Naba brought the mufflers out to the living room. She offered the cream one to my mother, her voice shaking with hope. She just wanted my mother to like her. Even once.”",
      "“What did she do?” Ma’un asked, his throat dry.",
      "“My mother looked at the uneven stitches. She took out a metal nail file from her purse. She grabbed Naba by the chin, pressed the sharp metal tip into her skin until it cut, and whispered: *‘You are just like your mother. A beggar trying to buy affection.’*”",
      "Ma’un’s hands clenched into tight fists.",
      "“My mother threw the muffler into the fireplace,” Sara whispered. “Then my father told the servants to lock Naba in the unheated storage room on the roof. In the middle of December. She was locked in the dark for three days with no food and no blanket. When they finally opened the door, her lips were blue, her chin was bleeding, and she was unconscious on the concrete floor.”",
      "Sara looked at Naba.",
      "“After that, Naba stopped asking for anything. She stopped speaking unless spoken to. She decided that staying invisible was the only way to survive.”",
      "The room went completely still.",
      "Cookie was curled quietly near Naba’s knees.",
      "Ma’un didn’t speak. He couldn’t.",
      "He looked at Naba. He looked at the tiny, faint white scar on the right side of her chin—the one he had noticed during their wedding photo.",
      "His vision blurred with tears.",
      "Before Naba could apologize, Ma’un leaned forward, wrapped his arms around her shoulders, and pulled her tight against his chest. He buried his face in her scarf and cried. He cried with deep, heavy sobs, his hands gripping her jacket like he was holding onto someone in a storm.",
      "Naba sat frozen in shock.",
      "Nobody had ever cried for her pain.",
      "Slowly, her hands came up around his back. She rested her forehead against his shoulder, letting the quiet warmth of his tears soak into the fabric.",
      "Sara sat by the table, stroking Cookie’s fur, wiping her own eyes with a napkin.",
      "“Good,” Sara whispered softly to herself. “You finally found someone who cares.”"
